<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Soran emirate</h1><p class="st-meta">1400 CE – 1836 CE (approx.) · capital Rawanduz</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Kurdish emirate based at Rawanduz that, under Mir Muhammad Kor, briefly became a powerful state with its own cannon foundry before Ottoman reconquest.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Soran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> ruled the mountains between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zab">Great Zab</a> and the Iranian frontier, in the north-east of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharaf-khan-bidlisi">Sharaf Khan Bidlisi's</a> 16th-century history, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharafnama">Sharafnama</a>, traces their line back to around 1400. For most of their history they were tribal lords under Ottoman suzerainty, competing with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/baban-emirate">Baban emirate</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bahdinan-emirate">Bahdinan emirate</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Soran reached its height under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-muhammad-kor">Mir Muhammad</a>, known as Mir Kor ('the blind prince'), who ruled from about 1814 to 1836. He built a standing army, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">cannon</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rawanduz">Rawanduz</a>, imposed order on the tribes and conquered neighbouring districts, including <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bahdinan-emirate">Bahdinan</a>, while his campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazidis">Yazidis</a> were notoriously brutal. The Sorani form of Kurdish takes its name from this region.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1836 an Ottoman army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/resid-mehmed-pasha">Reşid Mehmed Pasha</a>, supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> pressure, forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-muhammad-kor">Mir Muhammad</a> to surrender. He was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> and disappeared on the return journey, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> was brought under direct Ottoman rule.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1400 CE – 1836 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesopotamia and Iran</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:87.56%;width:8.68%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1400 CE – 1836 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rawanduz</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mir Muhammad Kor</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kurdish (Sorani)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Iraq</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Soran, Emirate of Soran</dd></dl></aside></div>

